          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does \"code first\" mean, and what actually goes wrong if the two are sequenced the other way round?",
          "answer": "The passages I retrieved do not define \"code first\" as a term, and they do not say what goes wrong if the order is reversed. The closest material is in the heart failure coding document (coding-heart-failure-i50). It says that when a record documents heart disease as hypertensive, or as due to hypertension, the combination code for hypertensive heart disease is reported first. A heart failure code from the I50 category is then added to show the type of heart failure. The document says the heart failure code is added rather than replacing the combination code. It does not explain the consequences of putting them in the other order.\n\nThe coding-type-2-diabetes-e11 document describes a related idea. Some diabetes codes pair a combination code, which names the association, with an additional code that gives detail, such as the stage of kidney disease. It does not describe a sequencing rule for reversed pairs either.\n\nThe retrieved passages also do not include the hypertension coding note that the heart failure document refers to. That note is where the rule for which combination code applies would be found. For the exact sequencing rule and its effects, you would need that note, or an official ICD-10-CM guideline, or a qualified medical coder.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
